(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-supraventricular-arrhythmias-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Arrhythmias I: Supraventricular Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has episodes of lightheadedness and one faint. A 24-hour ambulatory monitor shows runs of a fast, irregular rhythm that alternate with slow rates, and a long pause each time the fast rhythm stops. Which fast rhythm is most likely on her monitor?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Ventricular fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia","Atrioventricular nodal reentry"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fast rhythms alternating with bradycardia and offset pauses are tachy-brady syndrome, a form of sick sinus syndrome in which the tachyarrhythmia is most commonly atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation started an antiarrhythmic and then develops atrial flutter. Suddenly her ventricular rate matches her atrial rate at 280, with hypotension. Which complication is she now at high risk of?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation","A slow ventricular rate","Sinus arrest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. 1:1 atrial flutter, where every flutter wave conducts, causes extreme hemodynamic instability and carries a big risk of progression to ventricular fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with persistent atrial fibrillation, now rate controlled, wants to try to restore normal rhythm as an outpatient. Which is required before elective cardioversion?","choices":["21 days of anticoagulation","A permanent pacemaker","Stopping rate control drugs","An adenosine trial"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient cardioversion of atrial fibrillation is done after 21 days of anticoagulation, while rate control continues. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has had three recurrences of atrial flutter, and rate control has been hard to achieve. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Septal myectomy","Carotid endarterectomy","Catheter ablation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ablation is the definitive management of atrial flutter, which has a substantial recurrence rate and is harder to rate-control than fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 19-year-old swimmer has an electrocardiogram before a sports physical. She has no symptoms. The rate is 72, with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS, but the beats come closer together as she breathes in and spread apart as she breathes out. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus arrhythmia","Sick sinus syndrome","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A normal P wave before every narrow QRS at 60 to 100, with the rate rising on inspiration and falling on expiration, is sinus arrhythmia: a normal, physiologic rhythm that is irregular with a pattern. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 23-year-old competitive cyclist has a regular rhythm at 50 with narrow QRS complexes and no visible P waves on a screening electrocardiogram. He has no symptoms, and his echocardiogram is normal. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Stop training","No treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A junctional rhythm can be seen in healthy people with increased vagal tone, such as well-conditioned athletes, and needs no treatment when asymptomatic in an otherwise healthy person. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has inappropriate sinus tachycardia at about 110 after a full workup excluded other causes. Her only symptom is awareness of her heartbeat, and she asks whether she needs medication. Which response is most accurate?","choices":["She needs a lifelong beta blocker","Cardioversion will cure it","She needs anticoagulation","Usually left untreated; benign"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inappropriate sinus tachycardia is generally left untreated: patients tolerate the symptoms better than the treatment, beta blockers are poorly tolerated, and the prognosis is usually benign. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with atrial fibrillation takes warfarin because direct oral anticoagulants are not suitable for him. Which international normalized ratio is the target?","choices":["1 to 1.5","2 to 3","3.5 to 4.5","4 to 5"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If warfarin is used for anticoagulation in atrial fibrillation, the target international normalized ratio is 2 to 3. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man collapses while playing basketball and arrives with a very fast regular tachycardia. He is alert with a blood pressure of 112/70. A vagal maneuver fails, and adenosine slows the rate to reveal an irregular rhythm with wide QRS complexes, each with a delta wave (slurred upstroke). Which is the next step?","choices":["Intravenous atropine","Oral metoprolol","Intravenous procainamide","A second dose of adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When slowing from adenosine reveals atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White, intravenous procainamide is used and adenosine is never repeated: nodal blockade leaves the accessory pathway unopposed. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions despite cutting out caffeine and alcohol. She has no contraindications to beta blockade. Which treatment is first?","choices":["Metoprolol","Procainamide","Adenosine","Warfarin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Persistent, symptomatic premature atrial contractions are treated with a beta blocker such as metoprolol if not contraindicated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man with Wolff-Parkinson-White asks which rhythm would be most dangerous for him. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["Sinus arrhythmia","Atrial fibrillation down the pathway","Sinus tachycardia with exercise","Premature atrial contractions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atrial fibrillation conducting down an accessory pathway is the most dangerous combination: it can exceed 200, approaching 300, and cause collapse. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with known Wolff-Parkinson-White has palpitations. Her rhythm is irregularly irregular at rates up to 280 with wide, varying QRS complexes. She is alert with a blood pressure of 110/70. Which treatment must be avoided?","choices":["Procainamide","Electrical cardioversion","Later pathway ablation","Adenosine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In atrial fibrillation with Wolff-Parkinson-White, adenosine must never be used: blocking the normal atrioventricular node leaves the accessory pathway to conduct the fibrillation at rates approaching 300. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has abrupt-onset palpitations during a week of little sleep and heavy coffee. Her heart rate is 160 and regular, and every QRS is preceded by an identical P wave whose shape differs from her baseline P wave. She has no heart disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Focal atrial tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A regular tachycardia with one consistent, abnormally shaped P wave before each QRS, starting abruptly in someone without heart disease under increased metabolic demand, is focal atrial tachycardia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with a stable, regular narrow complex tachycardia at 180 is being treated in the emergency department. She is alert with a normal blood pressure. Which treatment should be avoided at this stage?","choices":["Vagal maneuver","Adenosine","A second adenosine dose","Unsynchronized defibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Unsynchronized defibrillation is for pulseless rhythms; a stable supraventricular tachycardia is treated with a vagal maneuver, then adenosine, and only an unstable one with synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy has a delta wave (a slurred upstroke of the QRS) with a short PR interval on a screening electrocardiogram but no symptoms. His parents ask what the finding means. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["An extra pathway causing fast rhythms","A sign of a prior heart attack","A normal athletic heart","A slow node needing a pacemaker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pre-excitation with a delta wave means an accessory pathway (the bundle of Kent), which carries the potential for reentrant tachycardia, as in Wolff-Parkinson-White. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 26-year-old marathon runner has a resting heart rate of 48 at a preparticipation physical. He denies dizziness, fainting or chest pain. The electrocardiogram shows a regular rhythm with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS and a PR interval of 0.16 seconds. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Stop running","Reassurance; no treatment","Atropine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sinus bradycardia is seen in healthy people as an adaptive response, particularly well-conditioned athletes, and an asymptomatic sinus bradycardia is not treated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man has recurrent regular narrow complex tachycardia episodes at about 170 that cause palpitations but have never made him faint. He asks when a rhythm like his would be likely to cause collapse. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["If it lasts over an hour","If the rate goes above 200","Whenever it exceeds 100","If he is older than 50"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Supraventricular tachycardia usually does not cause syncope or hemodynamic collapse unless the rate goes above 200, as in reentry tachycardias or atrial fibrillation with an accessory pathway. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with symptomatic sinus bradycardia at 45 also has hypertension that needs a new medication. Which antihypertensive should be avoided?","choices":["Verapamil","Lisinopril","Chlorthalidone","Losartan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Verapamil is a nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, and these drugs, like beta blockers and digoxin, cause sinus bradycardia; it would slow her further. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with known atrioventricular nodal reentry has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 178. She is stable. Which treatment almost always terminates it?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous procainamide","Catheter ablation","Vagal maneuvers or adenosine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atrioventricular nodal reentry almost always terminates with vagal maneuvers or adenosine; synchronized cardioversion is rarely required. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with decompensated heart failure and chronic lung disease develops an irregular tachycardia with three different P-wave shapes in one lead. What does this rhythm signal about her course?","choices":["A pacemaker is needed","A poor prognosis","A benign finding","Imminent recovery"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Multifocal atrial tachycardia is a poor prognostic sign, occurring in acutely ill patients with severe lung disease or heart failure and right heart stress. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has a regular tachycardia at 170 with an identical, abnormally shaped P wave before every QRS. Adenosine briefly slows the ventricular response, but the abnormal atrial rhythm continues unchanged afterward. Which rhythm does this response point to?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Focal atrial tachycardia","Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Orthodromic reentrant tachycardia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine is usually less effective at terminating focal atrial tachycardia than nodal or accessory pathway reentry, so persistence of an abnormal-P-wave rhythm after adenosine is the diagnostic clue. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with sick sinus syndrome has pauses on monitoring that alternate with runs of ventricular tachycardia. Which device is indicated?","choices":["No device; observe","Ablation alone","Pacemaker-defibrillator","Pacemaker alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tachy-brady syndrome often needs a permanent pacemaker, and when the tachyarrhythmia is ventricular tachycardia the patient needs a pacemaker with an implantable cardioverter defibrillator. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is admitted in an acute exacerbation. She is tachycardic and irregular, and her electrocardiogram shows three or more different P-wave shapes in one lead with a P wave before each QRS. Which is the most important treatment for this rhythm?","choices":["Treat the lung disease","Synchronized cardioversion","Catheter ablation","Adenosine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management of multifocal atrial tachycardia is treating the underlying condition, here the chronic obstructive pulmonary disease exacerbation; short-term rate control may be added in hospital. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has sinus pauses of up to 2 seconds at night on an ambulatory monitor. Her occasional dizziness happens only when she stands up quickly and never during the pauses. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Observation","Permanent pacemaker","Atropine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In sick sinus syndrome, management confirms whether symptoms correlate with the bradycardia; when they do not, observation is appropriate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a slow sinus rhythm at 38 six hours after an inferior myocardial infarction. She feels faint and her blood pressure is 80/50. Each narrow QRS has a normal P wave before it. Which drug should be given first?","choices":["Diltiazem","Adenosine","Atropine","Metoprolol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable sinus bradycardia, here from an infarction affecting the conduction tissue, is treated first with atropine, then a temporary pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman has new atrial fibrillation at 146 with palpitations. She is alert, her blood pressure is 132/84, and she has no chest pain or breathlessness. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Immediate ablation","Intravenous atropine","Intravenous rate control","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A hemodynamically stable patient in atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response gets intravenous rate control; rate control is the first goal. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with coronary artery disease has frequent premature atrial contractions (early beats with abnormal P waves) on a monitor. Which sustained rhythm may these beats go on to precede in him?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Complete heart block","Atrial fibrillation or flutter","Ventricular fibrillation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In patients with heart disease, premature atrial contractions may precede paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia, atrial fibrillation or atrial flutter, and frequent ones prompt a search for structural heart disease. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman has infrequent, well-tolerated episodes of atrioventricular nodal reentry that sometimes outlast her vagal maneuver. She does not want daily medication or a procedure. Which option fits her best?","choices":["Adenosine tablets at home","Lifelong anticoagulation","Pill-in-the-pocket beta blocker","Daily amiodarone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A 'pill in the pocket', a beta blocker or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker taken at home when an episode starts, is an option for nodal reentry. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with stable atrial fibrillation at 92 asks why the plan is to control his heart rate rather than shock him back into normal rhythm today. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["Shocks cannot treat it","His rhythm cannot be restored","Slowing the rate dissolves clots","It is the first goal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In hemodynamically stable atrial fibrillation the first goal is rate control, with anticoagulation; elective cardioversion can follow after 21 days of anticoagulation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has occasional premature junctional contractions (early narrow beats with no visible P wave) on a monitor and is anxious about them. He has no symptoms and no heart disease. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Metoprolol for life","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Reassurance; no treatment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. No treatment is needed if premature junctional contractions are asymptomatic and infrequent, and reassurance of the benign nature of the rhythm is often adequate. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"}]};
